   139	   It asks for the passphrase (the backup's key passphrase). `FPR-MATCH` and `RESTORE-OK` both, or this backup is
   140	   not good: redo S5 for it on a new stick.
   141	5. Eject in *Files*; remove.
   142	
   143	**S6. Public key to the out stick:**
   144	```
   145	O="/media/amnesia/<OUT label>"
   146	gpg --export --armor "$FPR" > "$O/root.pub.asc" && test -s "$O/root.pub.asc" && sha256sum "$O/root.pub.asc"
   147	```
   148	(`release.pub.asc` for B.) SHA-256 on the public sheet. Eject; remove. Nothing secret, and not the revocation
   149	certificate, ever goes on this stick.
   150	
   151	**S7. Move the key to the card** (only after S5 printed every `-OK`, including `RESTORE-OK`, for both sticks):
   152	```
   153	gpg --edit-key "$FPR"
   154	keytocard
   155	```
   156	Answer **y** to "Really move the primary key?", choose `1` (signature key), passphrase, admin PIN, then `save`.
   157	Then `gpg --card-status`: the signature key line shows your fingerprint. If not, see Failures (do not reset the card).
   158	
   159	**S8. Card policy, set and confirmed:**
   160	```
   161	gpg --card-status | grep -E 'Signature PIN|UIF'
   162	```
   163	- If it says `Signature PIN ....: not forced`: `gpg --card-edit`, `admin`, `forcesig`, `q`. Re-read: must say
   164	  `forced`. (`forcesig` toggles; never run it when it already says forced.)
   165	- `gpg --card-edit`, `admin`, `uif 1 permanent`, `q`. Then read it back in the machine form, which distinguishes
   166	  permanent from ordinary on (the human form prints `Sign=on` for both):
   167	```
   168	gpg --with-colons --card-status | grep '^uif:'
   169	```
   170	  The first number after `uif:` is the signature slot and must be **2** (permanent); 1 is ordinary on, 0 is off.
   171	  If `uif` is an unknown command or the value is not 2: **stop**. Do not proceed without fixed touch.
