   178	```
   179	2. No touch: run the signing command again (output `~/nt.asc`), enter the PIN, **do not touch**. It must fail
   180	   (timeout) and produce no signature.
   181	3. Fresh PIN: sign once more (`~/p2.asc`). It must ask for the PIN again. Note whether it did.
   182	4. `gpg --card-status`: record the signature counter (1 per successful signature).
   183	Copy `proof.txt` and `proof.txt.asc` to the out stick for the owner's verifier check.
   184	
   185	**S10. Shut down.** Remove the YubiKey.
   186	
   187	## Session 2: tests in fresh boots (no secret key in memory at start)
   188	
   189	Each in its own boot, same S0 checks (a new `GNUPGHOME`), YubiKey **not** inserted unless stated. A new boot has
   190	no variables: set them by hand each time.
   191	1. **Each backup** (twice: BK1, then BK2). Unlock only that stick in *Files*, then:
   192	```
   193	B="/media/amnesia/ROOT-BK2"                         # the stick actually inserted
   194	FPR=<the fingerprint from the public sheet, no spaces>
   195	gpg --batch --import "$B/secret.asc" && gpg --list-secret-keys --with-colons | grep "^fpr:::::::::$FPR:" >/dev/null && echo FPR-MATCH || echo FAIL
   196	echo restore-test > ~/r.txt && gpg --local-user "$FPR" --digest-algo SHA256 --armor --detach-sign ~/r.txt && gpg --verify ~/r.txt.asc ~/r.txt && echo RESTORE-OK || echo FAIL
   197	```
   198	   Record pass/fail on the public sheet.
   199	2. **The card alone**: insert only the YubiKey and one backup stick (for its `public.asc` only; import nothing
   200	   secret):
   201	```
   202	gpg --import "$B/public.asc" && gpg --card-status >/dev/null
   203	echo card-test > ~/c.txt && gpg --local-user "$FPR" --digest-algo SHA256 --armor --detach-sign ~/c.txt && gpg --verify ~/c.txt.asc ~/c.txt && echo CARD-OK || echo FAIL
   204	```
   205	   PIN, then touch when it blinks. This proves the card works without any residue of the generation session.
   206	
   207	**Acceptance:** S5 all `-OK`, S7 fingerprint on the card, S8 both policies confirmed, S9 steps 1–3 as expected,
   208	Session 2 all three pass. Anything else: the key is not accepted (see Failures).
   209	
   210	## Failures (do the safe thing first)
   211	
   212	| What happened | Do this |
   213	|---|---|
   214	| Any S0 check fails | Stop. Shut down. Nothing secret exists yet. |
   215	| A backup step prints FAIL | The key is still in memory. Do not continue to S7. Reformat that stick (or use a new one) and redo S5 for it. |
   216	| `keytocard` fails or the card shows no key after S7 | Read `gpg --card-status`. Retry S3/S7 once. Shut down only if both backups printed `RESTORE-OK` in S5 step 4: then the key survives on proven copies, and goes onto another YubiKey in a later session. If fewer than two did, do not shut down: redo S5 on new sticks first. Do not reset the card blindly. |
   217	| A PIN is refused | Stop; read the retry counter. Never guess down to zero. |
   218	| S8 cannot confirm forced PIN or fixed touch | Stop before S9. The card holds the key without its policy; record it; decide with the owner. |
   219	| S9 no-touch test signs anyway | Policy failed: the key is not accepted. |
   220	| A Session 2 restore fails | That backup is not good. From the other backup or the card, nothing is lost: in a new session, restore the good backup and make a replacement on a new stick (S5), then retest. |
   221	| Both backups fail in Session 2 but the card works | Keep the card; do not reset it. Stop and decide with the owner: accept a card-only key without backup (loss means a new key and a new release), or generate a new key. Nothing is published until decided. |
   222	| Both backups and the card unusable | Generate a new key; the old fingerprint is discarded and never published. |
