     1	# V1 service contract and failure model
     2	
     3	> **Status: OWNER DRAFT, 2026-10-19** (Claude Opus 5.5, owner instance). It restates what A9.1 and A10 already
     4	> registered. It changes no registered sentence; if it disagrees with A9 or A10, they win and this page is wrong.
     5	> Revised the same day after a full Codex review (`docs/reviews/v1-contract/CODEX-REVIEW.md`, VC-01 to VC-25, all
     6	> applied; `DISPOSITION.md`). Purpose: one page that the formal models, the conformance cases, the service and the
     7	> testers are all checked against, so that when two of them disagree we can see whether the contract, the model or
     8	> the code is at fault (the author's method, travelog 2026-10-19).
     9	
    10	## 1. What a client gets
    11	
    12	The client sends bytes. Tessera returns a **receipt**: a bundle that anyone can check later, without Tessera
    13	running and without our help.
    14	
    15	## 2. Guarantees
    16	
    17	**Conditions.** Every guarantee assumes the registered cryptographic, key-use, authority, chain-history and
    18	distribution assumptions (A9:32–52, A9:128–136). In particular: nobody misused the service's signing capability,
    19	SHA-256 and OpenTimestamps hold, and the checker's verifier release is authentic.
    20	
    21	When those hold, a checker who has the receipt, the published verifier and its trust configuration can establish:
    22	
    23	| # | Guarantee | Source |
    24	|---|---|---|
    25	| G1 | **Exact bytes.** The receipt binds these exact bytes. Change one bit and the check fails. | C1 |
    26	| G2 | **Presented to Tessera.** The bytes were presented to the Tessera V1 service at some issuance. | C2 |
    27	| G3 | **Both signatures.** Both of the service's keys signed. A missing, stripped or swapped signature fails. | C3 |
    28	| G4 | **The keys were Tessera's.** DNSSEC (`_tessera-v1.wamason.com`, at the snapshot's own anchored time) and a signed git commit (at its own evidence time) named the keys for the service's domain. The git commit was in the Bitcoin chain at least 6 blocks before the receipt. | C4, A10.6 |
    29	| G5 | **Existed by a block.** The signed bytes and both signatures existed before a specific Bitcoin block: the lowest-height qualifying block in the presented proof, confirmed 6 deep. | C5, A10.1, A10.5 |
    30	| G6 | **A real-time bound, under an unchecked assumption.** A-UTC says at least one of that block and its 5 successors has a time stamp at most 2 hours behind when it was made. If A-UTC holds, the bytes existed before U (the latest of those 6 times, plus 2 hours). U is reported separately and never changes the verdict. | A10.1.1 |
    31	| G7 | **Window, two checks only.** The declared issue time is not before the keys' window opened, and the chain time T is not after it closed. This does not prove the signing happened inside the window. | C4, A10.2 |
    32	| G8 | **No Tessera needed.** The verdict depends only on the inputs above. | C6 |
