docs/reviews/ceremony/CODEX-REVIEW.md:23:Pin and record the actual Tails, GnuPG, scdaemon and card firmware versions. Either use an offline-available, pinned YubiKey Manager with `ykman openpgp keys set-touch sig fixed`, or select and rehearse a GnuPG version supporting the printed command. Check fixed/permanent explicitly: ordinary `on` is weaker than the promised non-disableable policy and must not be accepted as equivalent at line 137. No online installation during the secret session.
docs/reviews/ceremony/CODEX-REVIEW.md:33:Add an explicit no-persistence boot condition, an empty GnuPG home in verified RAM used consistently by every command (including the revocation path), no internal mounts/swap/hibernation, no autorun, no NUC scripts/configuration, and authenticated image verification independent of the NUC. Name the signing hardware and complete the laptop credential/inbound-access check. Specify these conditions for restore boots too. No secrets, photos or transcripts may go to chat/repository/NUC. Stop if any isolation check fails.
docs/reviews/ceremony/CODEX-REVIEW.md:41:The export/copy commands run independently; `sync && ls` can succeed after `gpg` failed and left an empty `secret.asc`. Restore failure is only recorded, with no rejection condition. Scenario: both exports fail, keytocard fails, and the operator reaches shutdown. The sole usable secret vanishes. Another scenario leaves a working card but no recoverable backup and still proceeds to publication.
docs/reviews/ceremony/CODEX-REVIEW.md:43:Require successful exits and nonempty expected outputs for each export/copy, check the mounted destination is the intended LUKS mapper before writing, and require both isolated restore tests plus primary test before accepting custody. Quote paths and substitute the actual label for every backup, including the hard-coded `ROOT-BK1` in the restore example. A directory or filename existing is insufficient evidence of a backup.
docs/reviews/ceremony/CODEX-REVIEW.md:59:The listed custody record lacks the signing setup, laptop credential-check date/result, holder/access boundary, pinned software/firmware/encryption settings, reset-code choice (none is acceptable), and test outcomes. “AUTHOR ... never in the repository” also sweeps public selections such as image version, verification device and publication channels into paper-only choices. A future reviewer then cannot reconstruct the boundary from the dated record. Keep secrets and locations private; record the non-secret choices/results required to identify the procedure and setup.
docs/reviews/ceremony/CODEX-REVIEW.md:69:The theft statement at line 18 is also conditional: knowing a passphrase alone does not produce any key, and paper sheets/tokens must have compatible separation if location compromise is meant to expose at most one key. Separate boots on shared hardware do not withstand compromised shared hardware/boot firmware; retain the draft's peripheral assumption (`:146–149`). Registered basis: A10.4.2 `:624–643`; requester-specific draft basis `:92–98,203–204,260–289`.
docs/reviews/ceremony/CODEX-REVIEW.md:91:- **Hardware mode:** acceptable under A10.4.2, which also permits offline software custody. Backups deliberately remain exportable signing authority; PIN/touch do not protect their use. Every backup and its decryption secret belongs to the access-boundary analysis.
docs/reviews/ceremony/CODEX-REVIEW.md:92:- **LUKS2 with Disks defaults:** reasonable design but not a pinned setting as required by custody draft `:165`. Record exact tool/version, observed LUKS format, cipher/KDF parameters and protected secret-export format without secrets. The GUI description alone does not establish them. Do not treat this draft pinning rule as a registered demand for a particular cipher or two independent passwords.
